NORMALIZATION::
Normalization is the process of organizing data into related tables to reduce unnecessary redundancy and prevent data anomalies while maintaining data integrity

Normalization vs Denormalization
 Normalization                               Denormalization                         
 Reduce unnecessary redundancy     | Intentionally introduce some redundancy 
 Improve consistency               | Can improve read performance            
 More related tables               | Fewer JOINs in selected use cases       
 Common relational design approach | Used selectively based on requirements  



Q1. What is normalization?
Normalization is the process of organizing data into related tables to reduce unnecessary redundancy and prevent insertion, update, and deletion anomalies while maintaining data integrity
Q2. What is 1NF?
Atomic values and no repeating groups
Q3. What is 2NF?
1NF plus no partial dependency on part of a composite key
Q4. What is 3NF?
2NF plus no transitive dependency of non-key attributes on the key
Q5. What is partial dependency?
When a non-key attribute depends on only part of a composite key

Q6. What is transitive dependency?
When a non-key attribute depends on another non-key attribute
Q7. Why normalize a database?
To reduce redundancy, avoid anomalies, and improve data integrity and maintainability
Q8. Does normalization improve performance?
Not necessarily. It improves structure and consistency, but can introduce more JOINs. Performance depends on the workload and design
Q9. What is denormalization?
Intentionally introducing selected redundancy to meet performance or access-pattern requirements

Complete Zepto-Style Normalized Database
Step 1 — Customers(One customer -> One record)
CREATE TABLE customers (
    customer_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    customer_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    phone VARCHAR(20) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    pincode VARCHAR(10)
);

Step 2 — Stores (One store -> One record)
CREATE TABLE stores (
    store_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    store_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    city VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL
);

Step 3 — Products(One product -> One record)
CREATE TABLE products (
    product_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    product_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    category VARCHAR(50),
    price DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL
);

Step 4 — Orders
CREATE TABLE orders (
    order_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    customer_id INT NOT NULL,
    store_id INT NOT NULL,
    order_date DATETIME NOT NULL,
    status VARCHAR(30) NOT NULL,
    total_amount DECIMAL(10,2),

    FOREIGN KEY (customer_id)
        REFERENCES customers(customer_id),

    FOREIGN KEY (store_id)
        REFERENCES stores(store_id)
);

Notice: orders does not store:
customer_name
customer_phone
store_name
store_city 

-- Because those facts already belong to their respective tables

